If \( L \) has coordinate \( l \) and \( P \) has coordinate \( p \), the coordinate of \( M \) (closer to \( L \)) is given by: \[
M = l + \frac{p - l}{3} = \frac{2l + p}{3}
\]

Similarly, the coordinate of \( N \) (closer to \( P \)) is: \[
N = l + \frac{2(p - l)}{3} = \frac{l + 2p}{3}
\]

Thus, the value at \( M \) in this common case is: \[
\boxed{1}
\]
